      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an NMi OIML Certificate for a water meter (electromagnetic flow meter), type M5000, from Badger Meter.\n\nKey details:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03, revision 7\n- Project number: 3954718\n- Page total: 7\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 3 October 2025\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Scheme: A\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Applicant: Badger Meter, Inc., 4545 West Brown Deer Road, Milwaukee, WI 53224, United States of America\n- Manufacturer: Badger Meter Europe GmbH, Nürtinger Straße 76, 72639 Neuffen, Germany\n- Certified type: electromagnetic water meter, Type M5000\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Recommendation: R 49-1 (2013), accuracy classes 1 and 2\n- Documentation folder no. T10554-5 (this could be a documentation file in test_reports with role documentation_file)\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-13200198-01 revision 1, dated 7 July 2022, 29 pages\n- NMi-3531004-01 revision 1, dated 4 July 2023, 20 pages\n- NMi-3891220-01, dated 17 April 2025, 19 pages\n- NMi-3891220-02, dated 31 July 2025, 18 pages\n- NMi-3954718-01, dated 3 October 2025, 19 pages\n\nThese are \"associated reports\" — role: type_evaluation_report probably. The schema says role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. These reports established conformity via tests and examinations — I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for them, or \"test_report\". Hmm. The text says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are test reports / type evaluation reports. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all, since they support the Type Evaluation. Actually \"test_report\" might be safer given \"results of tests and examinations\". Let me use \"type_evaluation_report\" for them since they're associated with the OIML Type Evaluation Report mentioned on page 1... Actually the phrase \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" implies there is one TER. The listed reports are the associated reports. I'll mark them as \"test_report\" — hmm, but they could be TERs. I'll go with \"type_evaluation_report\" as these are the reports supporting the certificate under scheme A. Either is defensible. Let me use \"test_report\" for them since the text emphasizes tests and examinations... Actually, in OIML-CS scheme A, the certificate is issued based on the Type Evaluation Report. The listed reports constitute the type evaluation report (which may be split in multiple files). I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nAlso \"Documentation folder no. T10554-5\" — role: documentation_file, id: T10554-5.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nTable 1 General characteristics (type_level):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — this is also captured in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Could include as string value.\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — string. Maybe split? \"M1 / O\" with note. Keep as string value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Or value \"M1/O\", with separate note. I'll keep as string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" — water temperature class T50, range 0.1 to 50 °C. Could record: water_temperature_class: \"T50\", and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. I'll do that.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Record value 1.6 unit MPa, maybe a second characteristic max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 unit bar. I'll record both or one with unit_symbol \"MPa\" and a note. I'll do map_mpa: 1.6, map_bar: 16. Actually canonical label: \"maximum_admissible_pressure\". value 1.6, unit \"MPa\", and a second entry \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\" 16? Hmm, maybe better: maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and alternative in description? I'll add two: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative (bar)? That's noisy. Simpler: one characteristic with value 1.6 unit \"MPa\", plus one \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" unit \"bar\" value 16? Attributes should be distinct keys. I'll use maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent: 16 bar. Hmm, or just skip the bar. I'll include both with distinct labels: maximum_admissible_pressure (1.6 MPa), maximum_admissible_pressure_bar (16 bar).\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" — string.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — string / boolean true. I'll keep string.\n- pressure_loss_class: two variants: \"Full bore meter Δp 10 (0,10 bar)\" and \"Reduce bore Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\". This is configuration-dependent — full bore vs reduced bore. Could be config_level with axis \"bore_type\": conditions \"full bore\" → Δp10 (0.10 bar), \"reduced bore\" → Δp40 (0.40 bar). I'll add config_level entry: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore_type\". Values: {condition: \"full bore\", value: 10} with class Δp10... Hmm. The pressure loss class is Δp 10 meaning 0.10 bar. Let me structure: attribute \"pressure_loss\", axis \"bore_type\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: {value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\", ...}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.40 bar}]. And maybe pressure_loss_class separately. Config values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So value could be structured object. I'll do:\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"class\": \"Δp10\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore\", \"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"class\": \"Δp40\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, adding \"class\" key inside values isn't in the schema. The schema shows {condition, value}. Maybe I keep it simple: value 0.1 bar for full bore, 0.4 bar for reduced bore. Or keep pressure_loss_class as type_level string describing both. I'll do config_level with the numeric pressure loss and keep the class designation in matrix? Simpler: config-level attribute \"pressure_loss\" axis \"bore_type\". I'll include both \"pressure_loss\" and note classes via value string? Let me include two config attributes: pressure_loss (numeric) — good.\n\n- power_supply: \"Replaceable battery (2,9 – 3,7 V)\" → power_supply_type: \"Replaceable battery\", power_supply_voltage: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit \"V\".\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" — table 5 not in this fragment. Could include as string or omit. I'll include \"see table 5\" as string value? It's a reference, not a value. I'll omit or include as \"Software identification\" characteristic with value \"See table 5\". Hmm, rules say don't fabricate; a pointer isn't a value but it is what the doc says. I'll include it as a string value since it's meaningful. Actually maybe better to omit to keep clean. I'll include it — it does describe a characteristic (\"see table 5\"). I'll skip it to avoid noise. Hmm. I'll include with value \"See table 5\" — it's harmless and accurate.\n\nTables 2 and 3: flow characteristics per meter size for accuracy class 1 and class 2.\n\nColumns: meter_size, diameter_in_outlet (mm), q1 min (m³/h), q2 (m³/h), q3 (m³/h), q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1.\n\nTable 2 (class 1): DN50..DN600, 14 rows.\nTable 3 (class 2): DN50..DN600 with reduced bore variants for DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, 18 rows.\n\nThis is a model family: models differ by meter size (DN50...DN600) and bore type (full/reduced) and accuracy class (1/2).\n\nHow to model? model_family.models: one entry per variant: e.g. model_id \"DN50 class 1\", etc. That's 14 + 18 = 32 models. That's a lot but fine.\n\nAlternatively, model_id = DN size, and use config_level for class and bore_type axes. The schema supports matrix_tables for the 2-D tables and model_level for attributes varying per model.\n\nGiven tables 2 and 3 are separate by accuracy class, I could create two matrix_tables: \"Flow characteristics, accuracy class 1\" and \"Flow characteristics, accuracy class 2\". Then model_level entries attribute × model with models like \"DN50 class 1\"... That could be heavy. Alternative: models keyed by \"DN50\" etc., with matrix tables holding class-specific values, and model_level for q3 etc. per class as separate attributes? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest and follows the rules:\n\nRule 4: populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level, plus matrix_tables.\n\nModel variants: The distinguishing dimensions: meter size (DN50–DN600), accuracy class (1, 2), bore (full, reduced for class 2, and reduced bore exists only in class 2 table... wait, pressure loss class in Table 1 mentions both full bore Δp10 and reduced bore Δp40, but table 2 (class 1) has no reduced bore rows; table 3 (class 2) has reduced bore for DN50–DN100 only).\n\nI'll define models as: \"DN50/class 1\", \"DN65/class 1\", ..., \"DN600/class 1\", \"DN50/class 2\", \"DN50 RB/class 2\", \"DN65/class 2\", \"DN65 RB/class 2\", \"DN80/class 2\", \"DN80 RB/class 2\", \"DN100/class 2\", \"DN100 RB/class 2\", \"DN125/class 2\", ..., \"DN600/class 2\".\n\nThat's 14 + 18 = 32 models. For model_level, each attribute (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, diameter) × 32 values... that's big but complete. Alternatively use matrix_tables to hold the data and a smaller model_level. The schema example for R137 populates both fully. But 32 models × 6 attributes = ~192 value entries. That's a lot of JSON but acceptable? It might be very long. Let me consider.\n\nAlternatively, treat accuracy class as config axis: models = DN sizes (14 full-bore sizes + 4 RB variants = 18), and model_level values include class per model... but class 1 and class 2 tables have DIFFERENT flow rates for same DN (e.g., DN125 class1: Q1=1.6, Q3=400; class2: Q1=1.6, Q3=400 — actually same for DN125! Let me compare: \n\nClass 1: DN50: 0.315/0.504/63/78.75/200; DN65: 0.5/0.8/100/125/200; DN80: 0.8/1.28/160/200/200; DN100: 1/1.6/250/312.5/250; DN125: 1.6/2.56/400/500/250; DN150: 3.9375/6.3/630/787.5/160; DN200: 6.25/10/1000/1250/160; DN250: 10/16/1600/2000/160; DN300: 15.625/25/2500/3125/160; DN350 same as 300; DN400: 25/40/4000/5000/160; DN450: 39.375/63/6300/7875/160; DN500 same; DN600: 50.400/80.640/6300/7875/125.\n\nClass 2: DN50: 0.315/0.504/63/78.75/200 (same as class1); DN50 RB: 0.158/0.25/63/78.75/400; DN65: 0.5/0.8/100/125/200 (same); DN65 RB: 0.25/0.4/100/125/400; DN80: 0.8/1.28/160/200/200 (same); DN80 RB: 0.4/0.64/160/200/400; DN100: 1/1.6/250/312.5/250 (same); DN100 RB: 0.63/1.00/250/312.5/400; DN125: 1.6/2.56/400/500/250 (same); DN150: 2.52/4.032/630/787.5/250 (different from class1!); DN200: 6.4/10.24/1600/2000/250 (different); DN250: 6.4/10.24/1600/2000/250 (different); DN300: 10/16/2500/3125/250 (different); DN350: 10/16/2500/3125/250; DN400: 16/25.6/4000/5000/250 (different); DN450: 25.2/40.32/6300/7875/250; DN500: same as 450; DN600: 40/64/10000/12500/250 (different).\n\nSo flows differ by class for DN150+. Therefore models must encode both size and class. RB variants only in class 2.\n\nDecision: model_family.models with model_id like \"DN50/class1\". 32 entries.\n\nFor model_level, listing all attributes × 32 models would be enormous. The alternative permitted: matrix_tables preserve the tables; model_level \"attribute × model matrix\" — the schema says populate it. But given size, maybe I can scope model_level to the shared attributes and rely on matrix_tables for