ameter_in_outlet. That's 6 attributes × 32 models = 192 entries. That's really heavy. Given the matrix_tables already capture everything, maybe I can populate model_level fully anyway — the schema example did. But the response length is a concern. Let me reconsider: maybe I define models only by DN size (18 variants: 14 full bore + 4 RB), and treat accuracy class as a config axis in model_level? But flows differ by class for same DN, so model_level entries would need per-class values. The model_level values structure is {model, value} — can't have two values per model without splitting attributes like \"q3_class1\" / \"q3_class2\". That's ugly.\n\nCleanest: 32 models with size+class(+bore) in model_id. model_level full matrix. I'll do it but maybe trim: include q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 as model_level; diameter is constant per DN across classes, so diameter could be model_level too (same value for both class models of a DN) or part of matrix tables only. I'll include diameter in model_level as well for completeness. Hmm... 32 entries for diameter too. OK let's just write it out. Actually, to manage length, maybe I'll include model_level with all six attributes. Let me write them systematically.\n\nModel IDs (32):\nClass 1: DN50-class1, DN65-class1, DN80-class1, DN100-class1, DN125-class1, DN150-class1, DN200-class1, DN250-class1, DN300-class1, DN350-class1, DN400-class1, DN450-class1, DN500-class1, DN600-class1\nClass 2: DN50-class2, DN50-RB-class2, DN65-class2, DN65-RB-class2, DN80-class2, DN80-RB-class2, DN100-class2, DN100-RB-class2, DN125-class2, DN150-class2, DN200-class2, DN250-class2, DN300-class2, DN350-class2, DN400-class2, DN450-class2, DN500-class2, DN600-class2\n\nAttributes model_level:\n- diameter_in_outlet (mm): DN50→50 (both classes, RB 50), DN65→65, DN80→80, DN100→100, DN125→125, DN150→150, DN200→200, DN250→250, DN300→300, DN350→350, DN400→400, DN450→450, DN500→500, DN600→600.\n- minimum_flow_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n\nAlso accuracy_class could be model_level attribute: class1 models → 1, class2 → 2. That's derivable from model_id; skip? Might include for machine-readability. I'll skip since model_id encodes it. Hmm, actually including \"accuracy_class\" in model_level is redundant but harmless. Skip.\n\nType-level characteristics (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"? It's covered by recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2]. Include? Could add as string \"1 and 2\". I'll include in type_level as value \"1 and 2\"? It's not numeric; fine as string. Actually maybe better to leave accuracy classes only in recommendation. But Table 1 lists it as characteristic. I'll include it: attribute \"accuracy_class\", value \"1 and 2\". Hmm, structured value requires {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. value can be string. OK.\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Maybe split: value \"M1/O\", and note. I'll keep full string but clean: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — contains parentheses; fine as string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min:0.1, max:50}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 \"MPa\"; plus maybe \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative\" 16 \"bar\". I'll add both: maximum_admissible_pressure (1.6 MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Hmm, label uniqueness. I'll do:\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: {value:1.6, unit_symbol:\"MPa\"}\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent: {value:16, unit_symbol:\"bar\"}? That's fabricating a label a bit but it's a faithful representation of \"(16 bar)\". Alternatively one value can't hold two units. I'll include the equivalent. Actually simpler: skip the bar equivalent to reduce noise? It's part of the data. I'll include as \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative_units\"? Eh. I'll include \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" 1.6 MPa only, and mention 16 bar in... hmm. Rules say don't fabricate but representing (16 bar) as a second characteristic is reasonable. I'll include both with clear labels: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar (bar). Slight redundancy in labels but explicit.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" — keep string. Canonical label: flow_profile_sensitivity_class.\n- reverse_flow: true? The statement \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value true. I'll use boolean true. Or string. Structured value shape allows number|string; boolean not listed but fine? Schema says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. To be safe use string \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? Hmm. I'll use true — JSON boolean is fine even if schema doesn't enumerate. Actually safer to comply: value string \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll use boolean true; it's cleaner. The schema examples show value types as number|object|string. I'll use true anyway? Risky for validators. Use string \"intended (sensor measures reverse flow)\". Hmm. Let me just use value: true. Most validators using json.loads won't care. But instructions say schema value: number | {min,max} | string. I'll comply with string: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? That reads odd. I'll do value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — faithful. OK.\n\n- pressure_loss: config_level by bore_type:\n  values: [{condition:\"full bore\", value:0.1, unit_symbol:\"bar\", class \"Δp10\"}, {condition:\"reduced bore\", value:0.4, unit_symbol:\"bar\", class \"Δp40\"}]. The extra \"class\" key — not in schema. I could encode value as string \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\". Hmm. The value shape allows string. I'll do value as number 0.1 with unit bar and add the class into... maybe add a separate config_level attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" with values \"Δp 10\" / \"Δp 40\". Two config entries, cleaner. Yes:\n\n  config_level: [\n    {attribute: \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore_type\", values: [{condition:\"full bore\", value:0.1, unit_symbol:\"bar\"}, {condition:\"reduced bore\", value:0.4, unit_symbol:\"bar\"}]},\n    {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore_type\", values: [{condition:\"full bore\", value:\"Δp 10\"}, {condition:\"reduced bore\", value:\"Δp 40\"}]}\n  ]\n\n  \"Δp\" — avoid special char? It's fine, Unicode Δ. No LaTeX.\n\n- power_supply_type: \"Replaceable battery\"\n- battery_voltage / power_supply_voltage: {min:2.9, max:3.7} \"V\". Label: power_supply_voltage.\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" — include? I'll include as value \"See table 5\". Hmm, it references a table in a later page (part 2 fragment). Since the caller may merge parts, including a pointer is okay but as a \"value\" it's weak. I'll omit? The rules: \"Don't fabricate... Empty strings are not values\". \"See table 5\" is not empty. I'll include it — faithful.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: including it could confuse merging with part 2's actual software ID table. But schema has matrix_tables/footnotes for that later. I'll include the pointer string; harmless.\n\nRecommendation:\nid: \"R49\" — canonical. The recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". So id \"R49-1\"? Canonical form: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For part-specific: \"R49-1\". edition: 2013? The edition year 2013 — is that the edition number? R49-1:2013(E). I'll set id \"R49-1\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2].\n\nHmm, edition as int 2013 — the schema says edition (int). OIML editions are years for these. Fine.\n\nCertificate:\nnumber: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\"\nscheme: \"A\"\nproject_number: \"3954718\" (string or number? likely string with digits; 3954718 numeric. I'll use string \"3954718\"? Numbers rule applies to characteristics. Project number as string is safer.)\npage_