n\napplicants: as above.\nmanufacturers: as above.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"M5000\"],\n  \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n}\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"M5000\",\n  \"models\": [ 32 entries {\"model_id\": \"...\"} ]\n}\n\nMaybe add attributes per model like accuracy_class? The models[] entries can have attributes{} per schema (\"model_id, label, attributes{}\"). I could add attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"bore_type\": \"full\", \"meter_size\": \"DN50\"}. That duplicates model_level but makes models self-contained. That might actually be nicer: each model entry carries its class/bore. Then model_level bore_type optional. I'll add attributes to model entries: accuracy_class (1|2), bore_type (\"full\"|\"reduced\"), meter_size (\"DN50\"). Then skip bore_type in model_level (since it's in model attributes) and skip diameter? Diameter still useful as numeric attribute — keep in model_level. Let me decide: models[].attributes includes meter_size, accuracy_class, bore_type. model_level covers numeric flow attributes + diameter. That reduces model_level by 32 entries (no bore_type). Good.\n\ncharacteristics:\n\ntype_level: {\n  \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic flow metering\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1 and 2\", ...},\n  \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\", ...},\n  \"electromagnetic_environment\": {\"value\": \"E2\", ...},\n  \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", ...},\n  \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\": {\"value\":